// @run
// @log 1
// @log beta
// @warning Unused import `tag` (remove it)
// @hint redundant type annotation: `t` is inferred as `string`
// Two imports binding the SAME local name, which is the only shape that puts a
// DUPLICATE KEY in the module merge's rename map (`driver.vl`'s
// `modRenamePush`). A declaration colliding with an import is diagnosed by
// `modCheckDupBindings`, so this is the one program shape where the map holds
// two rows under one name — and the merge's readers DISAGREE about which row
// wins:
//
//   `modRenamed`     — VALUE references  — takes the FIRST matching row
//   `modRwTsName` / `modTypeRenamed` — TYPE positions — take the LAST
//
// That split is pre-existing behaviour, not something the sid-indexed map
// introduced: the linear scans it replaced returned at the first match in one
// reader and scanned on to the last in the other two. The map therefore keeps
// TWO sid views (`modRenameFirstBySid`, `modRenameLastBySid`) rather than
// normalizing the readers onto one answer.
//
// Both halves are pinned below, and that this case REDDENS for each collapse is
// a witness rather than a claim — both were compiled into a real compiler by the
// saved good seed and run:
//   - `modRenamed` reading the LAST view  → prints `2`, so `@log 1` fails;
//   - the type readers reading the FIRST view → `cannot assign string to 't' of
//     type i32`, so the case stops checking at all.
//
// The annotation on `t` carries the second half of the pin, so the `redundant
// type annotation` hint declared above is one to leave alone — take the hint and
// the case stops observing which module `Tag` came from.
//
// The declared warning records a THIRD, pre-existing disagreement: the
// unused-import lint names `first.vl`'s `tag` while `print(tag())` resolves to
// exactly that binding and prints its `1`. The lint's answer and the merge's are
// opposite here. It is orthogonal to the rename map and is declared rather than
// fixed, so the case asserts the whole observable surface instead of a chosen
// slice of it.
import { Tag, tag } from "./first"
import { Tag, tag } from "./second"

// TYPE position: the LAST row wins, so `Tag` is `second.vl`'s `string`.
const t: Tag = "beta"

// VALUE position: the FIRST row wins, so this is `first.vl`'s `tag`.
print(tag())
print(t)
